#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084452 :tutorial/content "There is a **linear correlation** between two sets of paired observations if the data points on the corresponding scatter plot are located along a straight line.\n\nWe say that the correlation is **positive** if the corresponding trend line has a *positive slope*. Similarly, we say that the correlation is **negative** if the corresponding trend line has a *negative slope*.\n\nWe have a **strong** correlation if the data points lie *close* to the corresponding trend line. Similarly, we have a **weak** correlation if the data points are scattered *far* from the corresponding trend line.\n\nLet's see a few examples:\n\n| ![](images/cd/cdd335fda9e91cbf7cdb05ad18065630985c50adb788df980708d822f7a5e8e9.png) | **Strong positive** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *positive* slope. The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | **Weak positive** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *positive* slope. The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | **Strong negative** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *negative* slope. The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| ![](images/07/076440b257a78474e9f1b3129ec448a407bb1b49215a29251d8e929cadaeb984.png) | **Weak negative** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *negative* slope. The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/ed/ed266d09a86d7200c02f004cd9bb3a87f75693462ff8846f7f3cc1ed4b66ed63.png) | **No linear correlation**:<br>The data points are scattered randomly. |" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d19b15d-f5c7-53da-b834-26b49ff2c356" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3698 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084490 :tutorial/content "The linear **correlation coefficient** $\\rho$ (the Greek letter rho, pronounced \"row\") is a real number between $-1$ and $1$ that shows the strength of a linear correlation. It has the following properties:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation\n- $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation\n- $\\rho = 0$ indicates no (linear) correlation\n\nThe approximate value of $\\rho$ can be determined from the scatter plot.\n\n| ![](images/09/09d585da995df4e5a67b022816eec246113171d3aad15dcb57d82c766dcb7d18.png) | For a strong positive correlation, we have $0.7 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1$. |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | For a weak positive correlation, we have $0.3 \\leq \\rho \\lt 0.7$. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | For a strong negative correlation, we have $-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq -0.7$. |\n| ![](images/92/92b65a115fd1621303a248de8ae03d05392827914f5200f267d368867d511ae5.png) | For a weak negative correlation, we have $-0.7 \\lt \\rho \\leq -0.3$. |\n| ![](images/2c/2cf1e2c3e79940229fc85c10d5bccdfed17afe0759cc897d53e5421328b21466.png) | For little or no correlation, we have $-0.3 < \\rho < 0.3$. |" :tutorial/id #uuid "c84adf99-1723-5b00-9eb9-b729a194dbe3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5951 :tutorial/title "The Correlation Coefficient"}]}
